WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.720
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:12.160
Today we are going to play with a magical AI&nbsp;
where we just sit in our armchair, say the words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:19.760
and it draws an image for us. Almost anything&nbsp;
we can imagine. Almost. And before you ask,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:22.960
yes, this includes drawing corgis too.

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:30.880
In the last few years, OpenAI set out to&nbsp;
train an AI named GPT-3 that could finish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:37.600
your sentences. Then, they made Image-GPT,&nbsp;
this could even finish your images.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:44.720
Yes, not kidding. It could identify that&nbsp;
the cat here likely holds a piece of paper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:52.400
and finish the picture accordingly, and even&nbsp;
understood that if we have a droplet here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:58.400
and we see just a portion of the ripples,&nbsp;
then this means a splash must be filled in.

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:06.640
And it gets better, then, they invented an&nbsp;
AI they call Dall-E. This one is insanity:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:10.000
we just tell the AI what&nbsp;
image we would like to see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:15.760
and it will draw it. Look. It can&nbsp;
create a custom storefront for us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:23.840
understands the concept of low-polygon rendering,&nbsp;
isometric views, clay objects, and more.

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:30.320
And that’s not all. It could even invent&nbsp;
clocks with new shapes when asked.

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:38.080
The crazy thing here is that it understands&nbsp;
geometry, shapes, even materials. For instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:44.640
look at this white clock here on the blue table.&nbsp;
And it did not only put it on the table, but it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:50.480
also made sure to generate appropriate glossy&nbsp;
reflections that matches the color of the clock.

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:57.360
And get this, Dall-E was published&nbsp;
just about a year ago, and OpenAI&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:05.040
already has a followup paper that they call&nbsp;
GLIDE. And believe it or not, this can do more,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:10.800
and it can do it better. Well, I will&nbsp;
believe it when I see it, so, let’s go!

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:19.680
Now, hold on to your papers, and, let’s start with&nbsp;
a hedgehog using a calculator. Wow, that looks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:26.240
incredible. It’s not just a hedgehog&nbsp;
plus a calculator. It really is using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:33.840
the calculator. Now, paint a fox in the style&nbsp;
of the starry night painting. I love the style,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:40.400
and even the framing of the picture is quite good,&nbsp;
there is some space left to make sure that we see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:48.000
that starry night. Great decision making. Now, a&nbsp;
corgi with a red bowtie and a purple party hat.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:52.400
Excellent. And a pixel art corgi with a pizza.

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:59.120
These are really good, but they are nothing&nbsp;
compared to what is to come. Because it can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:06.800
also perform conditional inpainting with text.&nbsp;
Yes, I am not kidding. Have a look at this little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:13.920
girl hugging a dog. But, there is a problem&nbsp;
with this. Do you know what the problem is?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:21.840
Of course, the problem is that this is not a&nbsp;
corgi. Now it is. That is another great result.

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:29.760
And if we wish that some zebras were added here,&nbsp;
that’s possible too, and we can also add a vase&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:38.160
here. Hmm…look at that. It even understood&nbsp;
that this is a glass table and added its own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:44.640
reflection. Now, I am a light transport researcher&nbsp;
by trade, and this makes me very, very happy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:51.200
However, it is also true that it seems to have&nbsp;
changed the material properties of the table,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:58.000
it is now much more diffuse than it was before.&nbsp;
Perhaps this is the AI’s understanding of a new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:05.280
object blocking reflections. It’s not perfect&nbsp;
by any means, but it is a solid step forward.

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:10.400
We can also give this gentleman a white&nbsp;
hat. And as I look through these results,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:15.520
I find it absolutely amazing how&nbsp;
well the hat blends into the scene.

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:23.360
That is very challenging. Why? Well, in light&nbsp;
transport research, we need to simulate the path&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:28.400
of millions and millions of light rays&nbsp;
to make sure that indirect illumination&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:34.560
appears in a scene, for instance, look here.&nbsp;
This is from one of our previous paper that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:41.280
showcases how fluids of different colors paint&nbsp;
their diffuse surroundings to their own color.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:47.600
I find it absolutely beautiful. Let’s&nbsp;
switch the fluid to a different one, and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:55.040
you see the difference. The link to this work&nbsp;
is available in the description below. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.040 --> 00:05:00.800
you see, simulating these effects&nbsp;
is very costly, and very difficult.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:07.120
But this is how proper light transport&nbsp;
simulations need to be done. And this GLIDE AI&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:14.240
can put in new objects into a scene and make&nbsp;
them blend in so well, this to me, seems also a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:20.720
proper understanding of light transport. I can&nbsp;
hardly believe what is going on here. Bravo.

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:27.600
But wait, how do we know if this is really better&nbsp;
than Dall-E? Are we supposed to just believe it?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:36.400
No, not at all! Fortunately, comparing the results&nbsp;
against Dall-E is very easy, look. We just add&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:42.800
the same prompts, and see that there is no&nbsp;
contest. The new GLIDE technique creates sharper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:49.600
higher-resolution images with more details, and&nbsp;
it even follows our instructions better. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:55.840
paper also showcases a user study where human&nbsp;
evaluators also favored the new technique.

00:05:56.480 --> 00:06:02.240
Now, of course, we are not done here, not&nbsp;
even this technique is perfect. Look. We can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:10.640
request a cat with 8 legs, and…wait a minute. It&nbsp;
tried some multiplication trick, but we are not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:18.160
falling for it. A+ for effort, little AI, but, of&nbsp;
course, this is clearly one of the failure cases.

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:24.640
And once once again, this is an AI where&nbsp;
a vast body of knowledge lies within,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:31.600
but it only emerges if we can bring it out with&nbsp;
properly written prompts. It almost feels like a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:38.000
new kind of programming that is open to everyone,&nbsp;
even people without any programming or technical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:44.560
knowledge. If a computer is a bicycle for the&nbsp;
mind, then OpenAI’s Glide is a fighter jet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.200 --> 00:06:50.160
Absolutely incredible. Soon, this might&nbsp;
democratize creating illustrations, paintings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:53.760
and maybe even help inventing new things.

00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:59.360
And here comes the best part - you can try&nbsp;
it too. The notebook for it is available in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:04.080
the video description. Make sure to leave&nbsp;
your experiment results in the comments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:11.440
or just tweet them at me. I’d love to see what you&nbsp;
ingenious Fellow Scholars bring out of this AI.

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:05.920
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

